<section image-layout="blank" id="rxV31XMYkJ" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="FVu-IYyoW6" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">Investment committee briefing</p><h1 font-size="40pt">Ozone Policy as a Supply Regime: What the HFC Phasedown Does to Cooling-Exposed Assets</h1><p font-size="lg">A fact base for screening chemical producers, cooling-intensive operators, equipment manufacturers, and refrigerant servicing exposure.</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><p font-size="sm">Policy, pricing, and diligence framework · September 2026</p></grid-item><grid-item id="ZhVT4ZmpeI" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/_K-nzvL7WUXdd9r4BR8zV.png" saved-media-id="4vqhbjkh1sv07bh" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="industrial-atmospheric-relief" alt="Precision editorial relief cross-section of atmospheric layers merging into a refrigeration compressor, cooling coil, recovery cylinder and control valve, built from matte mineral paper and oxidized metal under cool raking studio light, exposure blue, transition teal, restrained rust and charcoal on mineral white, no text, lettering or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="rpy-ex5j3g"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="wbOQvZbTsp"><h2>The ozone regime is now a government-administered scarcity machine, and the pricing effects are already in reported results</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="MkIK5GYB7P" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Situation</p><h4>The treaty machinery now governs climate gases</h4><p>The AIM Act phases down US HFC production and consumption 85% by 2036. The Senate ratified the Kigali Amendment in September 2022.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5UZVmDFNBX" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Complication</p><h4>Cooling demand is not capped</h4><p>Chemours TSS reached $568M of sales in Q1 2026, up 22% year over year; segment EBITDA rose 35% and margin reached 33%.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NowzBCT_zr" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Our resolution</p><h4>Follow three transmission channels</h4><p>Producer pricing power, input-cost pass-through in cooling-intensive assets, and reclaim economics are the channels to test.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Boundary:</b> no holdings, weights, valuations, or positioning data were supplied. Portfolio statements are hypotheses or diligence questions. Sourced facts read flat; illustrative arithmetic and our interpretations are labeled.</p><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://eelp.law.harvard.edu/tracker/hydrofluorocarbons-and-kigali-amendment-to-montreal-protocol/">AIM Act and Kigali tracker</a>, September 2022; <a href="https://www.contractingbusiness.com/industry-news/news/55376212/chemours-forecasts-strong-2026-cooling-season-refrigerant-demand">Chemours Q1 2026 results reporting</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="khMDEb6pBL" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="6FEEh4mTYB" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">Part I · Treaty credibility</p><h1 font-size="38pt">Part I — Why this treaty is the one regime that reliably binds</h1><p font-size="lg">Four decades of delivered schedules make policy dates behave more like a maturity ladder than a political forecast.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="R128GuM0xL" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/4LI8FnYt73RT024BmQh6d.png" saved-media-id="k038sitav7694u0" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="industrial-atmospheric-relief" alt="Precision editorial relief showing a vertical atmospheric cross-section above a compact refrigeration assembly, the ozone layer rendered as thin mineral-paper strata and the hardware as oxidized metal valves, coils and cylinders, cool raking studio light, exposure blue and transition teal with one restrained rust seam, no text, lettering or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="xdWnjI83AX"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="mfwP2-1KrL"><h2>The Montreal Protocol is the rare treaty that delivered—making its schedules a credible maturity ladder</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="hug" padding="14pt 16pt"><grid-item id="s53VX2hK1R" colspan="15"><p role="eyebrow" align="center">DELIVERY</p><h4 align="center">Nearly 99% removed</h4><p align="center">Almost all banned ozone-depleting substances have been phased out globally.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Ow9-J9jhlC" colspan="15"><p role="eyebrow" align="center">2025 SNAPSHOT</p><h4 align="center">29 September 2025</h4><p align="center">Ozone mass deficit was 36.7 million tons, more than 25% below the 1990–2010 mean of 50.1 million tons.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Eab7kdazDK" colspan="15"><p role="eyebrow" align="center">RECOVERY</p><h4 align="center">2040 · 2045 · 2066</h4><p align="center">Projected recovery to 1980 values: rest of world, Arctic, then Antarctic.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="FJ_29cZWnR" colspan="15"><p role="eyebrow" align="center">CLIMATE BENEFIT</p><h4 align="center">About 0.5°C avoided</h4><p align="center">Avoided warming gave the treaty machinery its second mandate.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="xSlWZWqzA5" background-color="surface" padding="12pt 16pt"><p><b>Our read:</b> schedules that have survived changes of government for four decades carry less political risk than most regulation and can be underwritten on their dates.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/ozone-layer-recovery-track-helping-avoid-global-warming-05degc">WMO and UNEP Scientific Assessment</a>, January 2023; <a href="https://wmo.int/resources/publication-series/wmo-ozone-and-uv-bulletin/no-4-sep-2026">WMO Ozone and UV Bulletin No. 4</a>, September 2026.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="JsKDGjMlDR"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="ijC_tvC7Lq" padding-bottom="24pt"><h2 font-size="28pt">Substitution cycles shifted value to owners of replacement molecules; phase-out dates vary by jurisdiction</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="VmnRzmC6Pd" colspan="15"><h4>CFC controls</h4><p>The 1987 Protocol began the substitution cycle.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="dVbBjrsc9L" colspan="15"><h4>HCFC bridge</h4><p>Lower ozone impact, with a long installed-base servicing tail.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="ctyh6lBu_L" colspan="15"><h4>HFC scale-up</h4><p>No known natural sources; GWP is hundreds to thousands of times CO₂.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="yGAUTiTzCX" colspan="15"><h4>Kigali reset</h4><p>The 2016 Amendment brought HFCs into quota-and-schedule controls.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="IWoTlzjCAk" cell-style="subtle" padding="12pt 18pt" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Non-Article 5 parties</p><h4>100% reduction from 1 January 2020</h4><p>0.5% HCFC servicing allowance through 2030.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="YIdgz_537g" cell-style="subtle" padding="12pt 18pt" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Article 5 parties</p><h4>100% reduction from 1 January 2030</h4><p>2.5% servicing tail, averaged over 2030–2040, until 1 January 2040.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Why the correction matters:</b> “HCFCs gone by 2030” overstates the cliff. Article 5 servicing demand creates a ten-year installed-base revenue window.</p><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/treaties/montreal-protocol/annex-c-group-i-hcfcs-consumption">UNEP Ozone Secretariat HCFC schedule</a>; <a href="https://www.epa.gov/hfcs/frequent-questions-phasedown-hydrofluorocarbons">EPA HFC phasedown FAQ</a>; Kigali Amendment, 2016.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="fJkEUckxcM" vertical-align="center" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="pHX5SyYWPa" vertical-align="center" colspan="18"><display size="lg">02</display></grid-item><grid-item id="o9KmhXu9Za" vertical-align="center" colspan="42"><p role="eyebrow">Part II · Policy machinery</p><h1 font-size="36pt">Part II — The machinery, and the 2026 divergence between the two largest markets</h1><p font-size="lg">The cap, sector rules, and reclaim requirements are separate levers. In 2026, only the timing lever diverged.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ofYbSmHN1P"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="qFC4-omg_o"><h2>Three levers do the work: a production cap, sector-by-sector transition rules, and a reclaim mandate</h2><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-straddle="true" spacing="medium" padding-top="34pt"><grid-item id="1kn88HWMUK" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/OYWyqEww03jKmCM56Dpd.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="tradeable industrial quota allocation" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A pie chart divided into sections of different sizes, symbolizing the allocation of resources or limits within a specific framework, embodies the concept of a quota, implying division, restriction, or assigned share.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Allowance allocation</h3><p>EPA sets the next year’s production and consumption allowances by 1 October. Allowances can be traded or transferred; EPA can withhold, retire, or revoke them.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="vFLMpXcqzL" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/oSKoAweSCcrvV30Ke7v4.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="sector transition schedule calendar" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black sector or a pie slice shape, often used to represent a part of a whole or a specific area of focus, similar to a slice of a circle or a wedge.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Technology transitions</h3><p>EPA can restrict a regulated substance fully, partially, or on a graduated schedule within a named sector or subsector.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="k96rHMpYbM" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/sEfYk1MWf7DRhP5jwnRy.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="refrigerant recovery and reclamation cylinder" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Since the image is a black shape on a white background, here's my attempt at describing the meaning of the image in a simple way that doesn't change the concept of reclamation:\n\nA small, solid black landmass or terrain in an expansive white space, symbolizing the concept of reclamation. \n\nHowever, I think I need a few more details about the shape to provide an accurate description.\n\nAssuming it's a shape that could resemble a piece of land, here's an alternative:\n\nA solid black shape, possibly resembling a plot of land or an island, in a vast white area, representing reclamation. \n\nIf you provide more context or details about the shape, I can give a more accurate description. \n\nFor instance, is the black shape geometric (e.g., square, rectangle, triangle), or does it resemble something more organic (e.g., an island, a peninsula)? \n\nWith more information, I can try to give a description that accurately conveys the concept of reclamation.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Reclaim and emissions</h3><p>Leak repair, technician, recovery, and reclamation rules turn waste refrigerant into regulated feedstock for the installed base.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Our read:</b> the cap creates scarcity, sector rules determine when demand is destroyed, and reclaim rules determine who can monetize the installed base.</p><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2025-10/9116-07-oar-cy2026-allocation-fr-notice-pre-published_1.pdf">EPA 2026 allocation notice</a>, October 2025; <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-05-26/pdf/2026-10387.pdf">US Federal Register</a>, 26 May 2026; <a href="https://www.epa.gov/hfcs/frequent-questions-phasedown-hydrofluorocarbons">EPA HFC FAQ</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="_CU8MOyUPc"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="fKUxeuUAMv"><h2>In 2026, the two largest markets split on sector timing alone</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="2JDhT02CAh" background-color="palette-2" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="outline">United States · Loosening</label></labels><h3>Sector deadlines moved</h3><p>The final rule issued 26 May 2026, effective 27 July, relaxed requirements for refrigerated transport, semiconductor process refrigeration and chillers, supermarkets, cold storage, laboratory equipment, and installation of pre-2025 residential inventory.</p><p font-size="sm">The reconsideration followed industry petitions and deregulatory measures announced 12 March 2025.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="nPG8yBEcPa" background-color="palette-3" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="outline">European Union · Tightening</label></labels><h3>Quota acquired a payment</h3><p>Regulation (EU) 2024/573 replaced the 2014 F-gas regime. Its quota system governs HFCs placed on the market, and quota for the 2026 allocation year onward is subject to payment.</p><p font-size="sm">The scope is HFC quota, not every fluorinated gas.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="a89Jqbgzv2" background-color="accent" padding="12pt 16pt"><p><b>Unchanged:</b> the US statutory phasedown still reaches an 85% reduction by 2036. The 2026 action changed sector timing, not the cap.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-05-26/pdf/2026-10387.pdf">US Federal Register</a>, 26 May 2026; <a href="https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2025-09/sept2025-tt-reconsideration-proposed-rule-summary-sheet.pdf">EPA reconsideration summary</a>, September 2025; <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX%3A02024R0573-20240220">EUR-Lex Regulation (EU) 2024/573</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="O2igWtPQAY"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="KsKVM1zFs3"><h2>The EU charge buys HFC quota—not every F-gas</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="3SboQR4AFn" colspan="35"><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="0" text-size="md" config="{&quot;legend&quot;:{&quot;position&quot;:&quot;none&quot;}}" width="500pt" height="220pt"><properties><property key="gas" label="Annex I HFC or blend" type="label"></property><property key="cost" label="Illustrative quota cost (EUR/kg)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>R134a · GWP 1,430</cell><cell>4.29</cell></row><row><cell>R449A · GWP 1,396</cell><cell>4.19</cell></row><row><cell>R452B · GWP 697</cell><cell>2.09</cell></row><row><cell>R454B · GWP 467</cell><cell>1.4</cell></row><row><cell>R455A · GWP 146</cell><cell>0.44</cell></row></data></graph><p font-size="sm"><b>Illustrative:</b> EUR 3 per tonne CO₂e × GWP ÷ 1,000. Applies only to Annex I HFC content and is incurred at quota allocation, not per sale.</p><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX%3A02024R0573-20240220">EUR-Lex, Regulation (EU) 2024/573</a>, Articles 16–17 and Annexes I–II; <a href="https://climate.ec.europa.eu/areas-action/fluorinated-greenhouse-gases/faq_en">European Commission F-gas FAQ</a>, 2025.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Zb99K4lNH7" colspan="25"><p><b>Rule.</b> Articles 16(1) and 17(5): HFCs placed on the market require quota; quota allocated for 2026 onward costs EUR 3 per tonne CO₂e.</p><p><b>In scope:</b> Annex I, Section 1 HFCs, alone or in mixtures.</p><p><b>Out of scope:</b> PFCs, SF₆, NF₃, and quota allocated before 2026.</p><p><b>R1234yf:</b> Annex II unsaturated compound; GWP 0.501; no HFC quota allocation payment.</p><p><b>Indexation:</b> neither the Regulation nor Commission FAQ states escalation or permanence.</p><p><b>Our interpretation:</b> Annex II’s commercial edge is exclusion from scope—not a measurable per-kilogram tariff differential; no “2,000-fold advantage” follows.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="frPR5WIlHy" vertical-align="center" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="i1oD9GAyqG" vertical-align="center" colspan="18"><display size="lg">03</display></grid-item><grid-item id="ocBmrYeoYY" vertical-align="center" colspan="42"><p role="eyebrow">Part III · Reported economics</p><h1 font-size="38pt">Part III — Where the money is visibly moving</h1><p font-size="lg">Producer results, refrigerant prices, and secondary quota trading show where policy scarcity is reaching reported economics.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="aUoOZtZNh1"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="FyfdOSmNIf"><h2>Four reported quarters show the phasedown becoming margin</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="aRNf8ufmcv" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">Chemours · TSS</label></labels><display size="md">+22%</display><h4>Q1 2026 sales growth to $568M</h4><p>Opteon sales reached $313M, up 12%; Freon reached $162M, up 67%. Segment EBITDA rose 35% and margin reached 33%.</p><hr color="neutral-border" width="sm"><p><b>FY2025:</b> record TSS performance, with Opteon sales up 56%, while group sales were flat at $5.8bn.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="k_Am3Snj3q" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">Solstice Advanced Materials</label></labels><display size="md">+19%</display><h4>Q1 2026 refrigerant-led growth to $711M</h4><p>Price and volume both contributed. Management attributed strength to the HFO transition and data-centre demand.</p><hr color="neutral-border" width="sm"><p><b>FY2025:</b> refrigerant net sales rose 16% on low-GWP adoption; group sales rose 3% to $3.9bn.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Our read:</b> regulated lines outgrow their groups; phasedown value concentrates here, not across every revenue line.</p><p role="caption">Sources: Chemours TSS Q1 2026 and FY2025 results (<a href="https://www.contractingbusiness.com/industry-news/news/55376212/chemours-forecasts-strong-2026-cooling-season-refrigerant-demand">coverage 1</a>, <a href="https://www.coolingpost.com/world-news/refrigerant-producers-record-sales-boosts/">coverage 2</a>); Solstice Advanced Materials Q1 2026 and FY2025 results (<a href="https://www.coolingpost.com/world-news/refrigerant-producers-report-strong-q1-sales/">coverage</a>).</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="wVoViipY7m"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="rctOG0ba2k"><h2>Legacy gas prices hold while the quota market clears far above the statutory allocation rate</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="vLvV-mvQqg" background-color="accent" vertical-align="center" padding="16pt 18pt" colspan="28"><p role="eyebrow">Secondary quota permit market</p><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="umarq_kWAl" colspan="25"><display size="md" font-size="44pt">€17.19</display></grid-item><grid-item id="WvlBsLpUU-" colspan="35"><h4>Q2 2026 average per tonne CO₂e</h4><p>Range: €14–€18.50; average down 2% from Q1.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="XACYjFUSGF" background-color="surface" padding="8pt 10pt"><p><b>Statutory HFC allocation:</b> €3 per tonne CO₂e.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm"><b>Our calculation:</b> secondary average ≈5× statutory payment.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="36W3R0cM4Y" colspan="32"><p role="eyebrow">Legacy and alternative gas price movements</p><table col-widths="[34,66]" header-col="true" borders="rows" striped="false" style="--table-cell-padding: 6pt 8pt;"><tbody><tr><td valign="middle"><p>R134a</p></td><td valign="middle"><p>Up 1% YoY; up 1% from Q1.</p></td></tr><tr><td valign="middle"><p>R410A</p></td><td valign="middle"><p>Up 1% YoY; up 2% from Q1.</p></td></tr><tr><td valign="middle"><p>Lower-GWP alternatives</p></td><td valign="middle"><p>From Q1: R290 +11%; R32 +9%; R448A +4%.</p></td></tr><tr><td valign="middle"><p>Reclaimed R404A</p></td><td valign="middle"><p>From Q1: service companies −9%; distributors −4%.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></grid-item></grid><p><b>Our read:</b> scarcity value, not the administrative payment, sets quota economics. Stable legacy-gas prices also show that the installed base continues to pay for servicing.</p><p font-size="sm">Sources: <a href="https://www.coolingpost.com/world-news/european-f-gas-prices-remain-stable/">Öko-Recherche European F-gas price survey, Q2 2026</a>; <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX%3A02024R0573-20240220">EU Regulation 2024/573</a>.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="rbVTHUUr4v"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="EP8olwwzCJ"><h2>Compute growth makes refrigerants a shared constraint</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="5LG5HqYDzX" colspan="36"><p>Solstice links data-centre growth to refrigerant demand. EPA’s 2026 reconsideration covers semiconductor refrigeration and chillers.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="lN1e4nIetq" colspan="24"><p role="eyebrow">Diligence question</p><p font-size="14pt"><b>Do infrastructure and chemicals share an untracked refrigerant-availability dependency?</b></p></grid-item></grid><grid marker="number" marker-size="small" marker-style="subtle" marker-shape="chevron" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-fill="true" spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug" padding="0.35em" style="--marker-chip-padding: 0.35em;"><grid-item id="LjDp52okHI" colspan="15"><h4>Compute build</h4><p font-size="sm">AI, data centres, and semiconductor capacity expand.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="c0H4HIk42s" colspan="15"><h4>Thermal load</h4><p font-size="sm">Heat removal rises continuously.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="KNLqGaSdDq" colspan="15"><h4>Cooling plant</h4><p font-size="sm">Chillers and process refrigeration need charge and service.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="SBUAR1ueDy" colspan="15"><h4>Demand meets the cap</h4><p font-size="sm">The build cycle post-dates assumptions in the 2020 statute.</p></grid-item></grid><table col-widths="[1,1,1]" header-row="true" borders="cols" striped="false" style="--table-cell-padding: 6pt 10pt;"><tbody><tr><td background-color="surface"><p>Ozone-depleting / HCFC</p></td><td background-color="palette-1"><p>HFC constrained</p></td><td background-color="palette-5"><p>Unknown</p></td></tr><tr><td><p font-size="14pt"><b>Legacy servicing exposure</b><br>Identify Article 5 installed-base demand and equipment replacement timing through 2040.</p></td><td><p font-size="14pt"><b>Quota and transition exposure</b><br>Identify refrigerant charge, service volumes, and dependence on sector-rule timing.</p></td><td><p font-size="14pt"><b>Evidence not supplied</b><br>Position-level gas inventories, replacement schedules, and pass-through contracts require diligence.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p role="caption">Sources: Solstice Advanced Materials Q1 2026 results, summarized <a href="https://www.coolingpost.com/world-news/refrigerant-producers-report-strong-q1-sales/">here</a>; <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-05-26/pdf/2026-10387.pdf">US Federal Register</a>, 26 May 2026.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Fn9foci0G3"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="SACvtGyt_8"><h2>Four break conditions—three are already observable</h2><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="CyffMbVorI" cell-style="subtle" padding="10pt 14pt" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Observable now</p><h4>Substitution moves beyond the incumbents</h4><p>Natural refrigerants carry near-zero GWP and no patent rent. R290 (propane) rose 11% quarter on quarter. Broad OEM standardization would compress HFO premiums.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="GNC71ae3xx" cell-style="subtle" padding="10pt 14pt" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Observable now</p><h4>Fluorine develops its own overhang</h4><p>The 2026 WMO/UNEP assessment terms name TFA for early identification. PFAS-adjacent action against HFOs would remove the Annex II scope advantage.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="uSu7LNITto" cell-style="subtle" padding="10pt 14pt" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Observable now</p><h4>US deregulation continues</h4><p>EPA proposed on 26 May 2026 to exempt road and intermodal transport refrigeration from AIM Act leak-repair requirements. Comments closed 10 July.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="s-y9oLnb-M" background-color="palette-2" padding="10pt 14pt" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Break condition · not observed</p><h4>Enforcement failure leaks scarcity</h4><p>Illegal imports or quota fraud would weaken scarcity. Existing EPA consequences show enforcement operating; sustained leakage would reverse that signal.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="lQUH3OTD2f" colspan="38"><p><b>Evidence requirement:</b> track OEM refrigerant standards, formal TFA or HFO actions, final US leak-repair rules, and enforcement volumes—not policy headlines.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="IbfjJWjF4B" colspan="22"><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://www.coolingpost.com/world-news/european-f-gas-prices-remain-stable/">European F-gas price survey</a>, Q2 2026; <a href="https://ozone.unep.org/system/files/documents/SAP_Presentation_36MOP_31Oct2024.pdf">UNEP Ozone Secretariat assessment terms</a>; EPA actions, May–July 2026.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="gj8lJiaB79"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="KZSW4TX1eW"><h2>Three scenario hypotheses—position data not supplied</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="LjtxDzSvEy" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="top-line" padding="12pt 14pt" colspan="20"><labels><label variant="solid">Base hypothesis</label></labels><h3>The schedule holds</h3><p>The statutory 85% US phasedown continues to 2036 with only marginal sector-timing slippage; producer margins keep expanding more slowly as reclaim volumes scale.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Test:</b> regulated-segment margin versus group margin each quarter.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0X4odbqEYI" background-color="palette-3" padding="12pt 14pt" colspan="20"><labels><label variant="outline">Bull hypothesis</label></labels><h3>GWP-linked pricing spreads</h3><p>Another jurisdiction adopts an EU-modeled quota payment, widening the cost wedge and improving reclaim economics where recycled material sits outside quota.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Test:</b> a second jurisdiction legislates a quota charge.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="ExBrfakj9R" background-color="palette-2" padding="12pt 14pt" colspan="20"><labels><label variant="outline">Bear hypothesis</label></labels><h3>Naturals meet fluorine action</h3><p>Propane and CO₂ OEM standards converge with fluorinated-chemistry rules, compressing replacement-molecule pricing toward commodity economics while legacy supply remains constrained.</p><p font-size="sm"><b>Test:</b> OEM standards and formal HFO or TFA action.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="O66YJKs5js" background-color="surface" padding="10pt 12pt" colspan="36"><p><b>Open question:</b> De-rating versus franchise impairment depends on holdings, entry valuation, and duration—not provided.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5yeT-2gvR0" padding="10pt 0" colspan="24"><p font-size="sm"><b>Policy anchors:</b> US AIM Act phasedown to 2036; European Commission Regulation (EU) 2024/573. Effects and tests are hypotheses.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="vkiGxklxO9"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="NnDJZ2b4CN"><h2>Three decisions today: facts first, positioning second</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="oVpoiIyj66" colspan="20"><h4>Commission exposure screen</h4><p>Map producers, cooling-intensive operators, equipment OEMs, reclaim and servicing. Deliver names, weights, transmission channels, and ODS / HFC / unknown classifications.</p><p font-size="sm">Owner: [name] · Date: [date]</p></grid-item><grid-item id="VpLhjqrthR" colspan="20"><h4>Quantify pass-through</h4><p>For cold chain, food retail and data centres, estimate servicing cost per unit of capacity. Separate EU quota-linked cost from refrigerant market price.</p><p font-size="sm">Owner: [name] · Date: [date]</p></grid-item><grid-item id="xcK7LzlnW9" colspan="20"><h4>Scope reclaim before sizing</h4><p>Confirm quota treatment by jurisdiction, then test certification, collection, yield and customer economics before any capital discussion.</p><p font-size="sm">Owner: [name] · Date: [date]</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="2kUADODzVn" background-color="palette-2" padding="10pt 18pt"><p><b>Trip-wires:</b> formal EU or EPA action naming TFA or HFOs under a PFAS framework; natural-refrigerant standardization by two of the top five HVAC OEMs; or a sustained move in EU secondary quota permit prices outside €14 to €18.50 per tonne CO₂e.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="6ppJi06LFb" colspan="30"><p font-size="14pt"><b>Management questions:</b> current charge inventory by gas; equipment replacement schedule; contractual ability to pass servicing cost through.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="2XPkOdFf8R" colspan="30"><p font-size="sm">Sources: European Commission and EPA policy frameworks; <a href="https://www.coolingpost.com/world-news/european-f-gas-prices-remain-stable/">Öko-Recherche European F-gas price survey</a>, Q2 2026. Owners and dates deliberately unfilled.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>